12:["$","p",null,{"children":["What it adds on top of the base image is small: a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge"}]," user (uid\n1000) and home directory, a world-writable ",["$","code",null,{"children":"/tmp"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"/usr/bin/env"}],", and\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"coreutils"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"bash"}]," and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"gnused"}]," on ",["$","code",null,{"children":"/bin"}],". It keeps the base image's tag so\nthe worker image and the toolchain it wraps stay visibly paired. It does\n",["$","em",null,{"children":"not"}]," bake the ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge"}]," binary in, whatever its header comment says: the\nKubernetes examples inject the binary at pod start with the\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge-worker-init"}]," init container, which copies it into a shared\nvolume, and the worker container then runs ",["$","code",null,{"children":"/shared/forge /worker.json5"}],".\nThat keeps the toolchain image and the Forge version independently\nupgradable."]}]
